WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.951
Local LLM workloads vary widely.

00:00:01.951 --> 00:00:05.282
Interactive chat is latency-sensitive because a person is waiting.

00:00:05.282 --> 00:00:09.723
Coding agents may issue many sequential calls while reading files, proposing edits,

00:00:09.723 --> 00:00:10.464
and retrying.

00:00:10.464 --> 00:00:14.165
Batch summarization can tolerate longer waits but may create bursts.

00:00:14.165 --> 00:00:16.016
Document analysis stresses context length.

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:19.347
Embedding jobs may use different models and endpoints entirely.

00:00:19.347 --> 00:00:22.678
Short requests and long-context requests create different routing needs.

00:00:22.678 --> 00:00:25.269
A quick prompt may finish in seconds.

00:00:25.269 --> 00:00:30.451
A large document prompt may spend significant time in prompt processing before generation begins.

00:00:30.451 --> 00:00:33.042
If those requests are treated as identical,

00:00:33.042 --> 00:00:37.483
one backend can become clogged with long jobs while another could have

00:00:37.483 --> 00:00:39.334
served short interactive work quickly.

00:00:39.334 --> 00:00:42.665
Concurrency turns simple user behavior into real capacity pressure.

00:00:42.665 --> 00:00:46.367
Several people may each think they are making one request,

00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:49.698
while the service sees overlapping prompt processing, token generation,

00:00:49.698 --> 00:00:50.808
and streaming connections.

00:00:50.808 --> 00:00:55.620
A coding assistant or tool-using agent may keep a backend busy for minutes.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:01.542
Routing should consider how much work each server can accept before queueing or rejecting new requests.

00:01:01.542 --> 00:01:03.763
Session continuity and retries also matter.

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:09.315
Stateless clients send full context with each request and can route to any compatible backend.

00:01:09.315 --> 00:01:12.276
Other workflows rely on cache behavior, temporary state,

00:01:12.276 --> 00:01:15.237
or tool context and may need sticky sessions.

00:01:15.237 --> 00:01:18.198
Retries can improve reliability after a backend failure,

00:01:18.198 --> 00:01:23.380
but blind retries are risky when a request triggered actions or external tool use.
